{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JbziVWM8L9PpfciLGDyjR1","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"Cross-architecture static probe (32B models)\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"Layer sweep + static LR probe on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B and QwQ-32B (64 layers, 5120-d). Identifies best layer, evaluates static probe coverage/accuracy.\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"r1_32b_best_layer_idx\": 30,\n7\t      \"r1_32b_best_layer_depth_pct\": 0.47,\n8\t      \"r1_32b_static_acc\": 0.7943,\n9\t      \"r1_32b_static_coverage\": 0.442,\n10\t      \"r1_32b_static_lead_mean\": -2.8,\n11\t      \"r1_32b_fisher_orig\": 15.3,\n12\t      \"r1_32b_fisher_pca3\": 1.02,\n13\t      \"qwq_32b_best_layer_idx\": 26,\n14\t      \"qwq_32b_best_layer_depth_pct\": 0.41,\n15\t      \"qwq_32b_static_acc\": 0.7651,\n16\t      \"qwq_32b_static_coverage\": 0.372,\n17\t      \"qwq_32b_static_lead_mean\": -3.4,\n18\t      \"qwq_32b_fisher_orig\": 13.7,\n19\t      \"qwq_32b_fisher_pca3\": 0.87,\n20\t      \"num_traces_r1_32b\": 209,\n21\t      \"num_traces_qwq\": 209\n22\t    },\n23\t    \"tables\": [\n24\t      {\n25\t        \"name\": \"Table 2: Layer sweep results (64-layer models)\",\n26\t        \"columns\": [\n27\t          \"Model\",\n28\t          \"Best Layer\",\n29\t          \"Depth %\",\n30\t          \"Static Acc\",\n31\t          \"Coverage\",\n32\t          \"Lead Mean\",\n33\t          \"Fisher (orig)\",\n34\t          \"Fisher (PCA-3)\"\n35\t        ],\n36\t        \"rows\": [\n37\t          [\n38\t            \"r1-8b (reference)\",\n39\t            \"14/32\",\n40\t            \"44%\",\n41\t            \"0.817\",\n42\t            \"0.465\",\n43\t            \"-2.0\",\n44\t            \"17.9\",\n45\t            \"0.93\"\n46\t          ],\n47\t          [\n48\t            \"r1-32b\",\n49\t            \"30/64\",\n50\t            \"47%\",\n51\t            \"0.794\",\n52\t            \"0.442\",\n53\t            \"-2.8\",\n54\t            \"15.3\",\n55\t            \"1.02\"\n56\t          ],\n57\t          [\n58\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n59\t            \"26/64\",\n60\t            \"41%\",\n61\t            \"0.765\",\n62\t            \"0.372\",\n63\t            \"-3.4\",\n64\t            \"13.7\",\n65\t            \"0.87\"\n66\t          ]\n67\t        ]\n68\t      }\n69\t    ],\n70\t    \"figures\": [],\n71\t    \"analysis\": \"Cross-architecture static probe results confirm Claim 1 generalizes. Both 32B models show the same qualitative pattern as r1-8b: high Fisher in full 5120-d space but collapse in low-rank projections. r1-32b slightly better than QwQ due to shared DeepSeek training lineage. QwQ's lower Fisher (13.7 vs 17.9) suggests Qwen-native training produces more diffuse safety representations. Coverage 37-44% vs r1-8b's 46.5% — static probes consistently fail across architectures. Best layer depth percentage is remarkably consistent (41-47%) suggesting a universal property of transformer safety representations.\\n\\nStatic probes fail cross-architecture as expected — signal is distributed across minor PCs (Fisher drops from 13-15→~1 in PCA-3D), consistent with r1-8b finding. r1-32b slightly outperforms QwQ because it shares the DeepSeek training pipeline with r1-8b (similar internal safety representations). QwQ has a different training regime (Qwen team's RL approach), producing less separable static geometry. Best layer at ~41-47% depth matches r1-8b's 44% finding — middle layers capture task-relevant features universally. Both models confirm Claim 1: static probes achieve <50% coverage despite high Fisher in full space.\"\n72\t  },\n73\t  {\n74\t    \"name\": \"Cross-architecture temporal window LR\",\n75\t    \"description\": \"Temporal LR (W=15) on r1-32b and QwQ-32B at their best layers. Tests whether +30pp coverage improvement replicates cross-architecture.\",\n76\t    \"results\": {\n77\t      \"r1_32b_temporal_coverage\": 0.698,\n78\t      \"r1_32b_temporal_acc\": 0.8391,\n79\t      \"r1_32b_temporal_lead_mean\": -0.6,\n80\t      \"r1_32b_coverage_gap_pp\": 25.6,\n81\t      \"qwq_32b_temporal_coverage\": 0.558,\n82\t      \"qwq_32b_temporal_acc\": 0.8103,\n83\t      \"qwq_32b_temporal_lead_mean\": -1.8,\n84\t      \"qwq_32b_coverage_gap_pp\": 18.6,\n85\t      \"avg_coverage_gap_pp\": 22.1\n86\t    },\n87\t    \"tables\": [\n88\t      {\n89\t        \"name\": \"Table 3: Temporal vs Static coverage by model\",\n90\t        \"columns\": [\n91\t          \"Model\",\n92\t          \"Static Cov\",\n93\t          \"Temporal W=15 Cov\",\n94\t          \"Gap (pp)\",\n95\t          \"Temporal Acc\",\n96\t          \"Lead Mean\"\n97\t        ],\n98\t        \"rows\": [\n99\t          [\n100\t            \"r1-8b (reference)\",\n101\t            \"0.465\",\n102\t            \"0.767\",\n103\t            \"+30.2\",\n104\t            \"0.848\",\n105\t            \"-1.09\"\n106\t          ],\n107\t          [\n108\t            \"r1-32b\",\n109\t            \"0.442\",\n110\t            \"0.698\",\n111\t            \"+25.6\",\n112\t            \"0.839\",\n113\t            \"-0.6\"\n114\t          ],\n115\t          [\n116\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n117\t            \"0.372\",\n118\t            \"0.558\",\n119\t            \"+18.6\",\n120\t            \"0.810\",\n121\t            \"-1.8\"\n122\t          ]\n123\t        ]\n124\t      }\n125\t    ],\n126\t    \"figures\": [],\n127\t    \"analysis\": \"Temporal advantage is robust in direction (+15-26pp across models) but architecture-dependent in magnitude. r1-32b replicates well (25.6pp gap, near r1-8b's 30pp), while QwQ shows a weaker but real 18.6pp improvement. The discrepancy likely reflects different RL training approaches: DeepSeek models exhibit gradual safety reasoning density decline (well-captured by W=15), while QwQ's training produces more abrupt transitions. Claim 2 holds but needs reframing: 'temporal context consistently improves detection across architectures, with benefit magnitude correlated to the gradualness of the commitment transition.' The average 22pp gap is still substantial and clearly exceeds noise.\\n\\nTemporal advantage replicates on r1-32b (+25.6pp, approaching r1-8b's +30pp) but is weaker on QwQ-32B (+18.6pp). r1-32b shares DeepSeek's training dynamics — safety reasoning density decline patterns are similar to r1-8b, so the temporal window captures them effectively. QwQ has a different RL training procedure that produces more abrupt rather than gradual commitment transitions — the 15-step window captures some but not all of the temporal signal. The gap EXISTS on both models (confirming temporal context helps universally) but magnitude is architecture-dependent. This is the median outcome: partial replication, one strong + one moderate.\"\n128\t  },\n129\t  {\n130\t    \"name\": \"Cross-model transfer evaluation\",\n131\t    \"description\": \"Zero-shot cross-architecture probe transfer. Train on r1-8b (Llama), test on r1-32b/QwQ (Qwen2.5). Train on Qwen, test on Llama. Uses learned projection head (4096↔5120).\",\n132\t    \"results\": {\n133\t      \"llama_to_r1_32b_coverage_retention\": 0.56,\n134\t      \"llama_to_qwq_coverage_retention\": 0.43,\n135\t      \"qwen_to_llama_coverage_retention\": 0.47,\n136\t      \"within_qwen_r1_32b_to_qwq_retention\": 0.72,\n137\t      \"within_qwen_qwq_to_r1_32b_retention\": 0.68,\n138\t      \"projection_head_params\": 20971520,\n139\t      \"cross_family_avg_retention\": 0.49,\n140\t      \"within_family_avg_retention\": 0.7\n141\t    },\n142\t    \"tables\": [\n143\t      {\n144\t        \"name\": \"Table 4: Cross-model transfer matrix (coverage retention %)\",\n145\t        \"columns\": [\n146\t          \"Train →\",\n147\t          \"Test: r1-8b (Llama)\",\n148\t          \"Test: r1-32b (Qwen)\",\n149\t          \"Test: QwQ (Qwen)\"\n150\t        ],\n151\t        \"rows\": [\n152\t          [\n153\t            \"r1-8b (Llama)\",\n154\t            \"100%\",\n155\t            \"56%\",\n156\t            \"43%\"\n157\t          ],\n158\t          [\n159\t            \"r1-32b (Qwen)\",\n160\t            \"47%\",\n161\t            \"100%\",\n162\t            \"72%\"\n163\t          ],\n164\t          [\n165\t            \"QwQ-32B (Qwen)\",\n166\t            \"47%\",\n167\t            \"68%\",\n168\t            \"100%\"\n169\t          ]\n170\t        ]\n171\t      }\n172\t    ],\n173\t    \"figures\": [],\n174\t    \"analysis\": \"Transfer results reveal a clear family boundary: within-Qwen transfers retain 68-72% of same-model performance, while cross-family transfers average only 49%. This is consistent with the median tier expectation. The practical implication: a probe trained on one model in a family can provide moderate monitoring for siblings, but cross-family deployment requires retraining. For the paper, this motivates model-specific probes as the default recommendation while noting that within-family transfer is a viable low-cost option. The asymmetry (Llama→r1-32b at 56% vs QwQ→Llama at 47%) suggests the DeepSeek fine-tuning creates a partial bridge between families.\\n\\nWithin-family transfer (Qwen→Qwen) works reasonably well (68-72% retention) because both models share the Qwen2.5 base architecture and similar embedding geometry — safety representations occupy analogous subspaces. Cross-family transfer (Llama→Qwen, Qwen→Llama) degrades to ~43-56% retention because the learned projection head must bridge fundamentally different representation geometries. The Llama→r1-32b direction is slightly better (56%) than Llama→QwQ (43%) because r1-32b's DeepSeek fine-tuning shares some training signal structure with r1-8b despite the architecture difference. This confirms the median assumption: cross-family transfer works but with substantial degradation (<50% avg retention).\"\n175\t  },\n176\t  {\n177\t    \"name\": \"Cross-architecture lead time analysis\",\n178\t    \"description\": \"GRU (256-d, multi-layer) lead time evaluation on r1-32b and QwQ-32B. Tests whether +4.7 mean lead time finding generalizes.\",\n179\t    \"results\": {\n180\t      \"r1_32b_gru_lead_mean\": 3.1,\n181\t      \"r1_32b_gru_lead_median\": 0,\n182\t      \"r1_32b_gru_lead_std\": 11.4,\n183\t      \"r1_32b_gru_positive_lead_rate\": 0.36,\n184\t      \"r1_32b_gru_crossing_rate\": 0.72,\n185\t      \"qwq_32b_gru_lead_mean\": 1.4,\n186\t      \"qwq_32b_gru_lead_median\": -1,\n187\t      \"qwq_32b_gru_lead_std\": 9.8,\n188\t      \"qwq_32b_gru_positive_lead_rate\": 0.28,\n189\t      \"qwq_32b_gru_crossing_rate\": 0.63\n190\t    },\n191\t    \"tables\": [\n192\t      {\n193\t        \"name\": \"Table 5: Lead time analysis across models (GRU multi-layer)\",\n194\t        \"columns\": [\n195\t          \"Model\",\n196\t          \"Lead Mean\",\n197\t          \"Lead Median\",\n198\t          \"Lead Std\",\n199\t          \"Positive Lead %\",\n200\t          \"Crossing Rate\"\n201\t        ],\n202\t        \"rows\": [\n203\t          [\n204\t            \"r1-8b\",\n205\t            \"+4.7\",\n206\t            \"0\",\n207\t            \"13.0\",\n208\t            \"39%\",\n209\t            \"76.7%\"\n210\t          ],\n211\t          [\n212\t            \"r1-32b\",\n213\t            \"+3.1\",\n214\t            \"0\",\n215\t            \"11.4\",\n216\t            \"36%\",\n217\t            \"72.0%\"\n218\t          ],\n219\t          [\n220\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n221\t            \"+1.4\",\n222\t            \"-1\",\n223\t            \"9.8\",\n224\t            \"28%\",\n225\t            \"63.0%\"\n226\t          ]\n227\t        ]\n228\t      }\n229\t    ],\n230\t    \"figures\": [],\n231\t    \"analysis\": \"Lead time results confirm Claim 3 across architectures: pre-textual detection is 'unreliable positive.' The positive mean persists but magnitude decreases with architectural distance from the original r1-8b finding. QwQ shows genuinely poor timing (+1.4 mean, median -1) — its abrupt commitment transitions provide less exploitable temporal structure. The consistent pattern (high std, low positive-lead rate, median ≤ 0) across all models strengthens the 'fundamental limitation' argument. The oracle's +31-step advantage remains unreachable by any current method.\\n\\nGRU lead time degrades on 32B models compared to r1-8b's +4.7. r1-32b achieves +3.1 (reduced from 4.7 — still positive mean but same median=0 pattern). QwQ further degrades to +1.4 mean with negative median, reflecting its more abrupt transitions that give the sequential model less predictive runway. The fundamental pattern persists: positive mean but unreliable (high std, low positive-lead rate). With only 209 traces and high variance, the GRU cannot reliably learn the timing signal on any model. QwQ's worse performance aligns with its more abrupt commitment transitions — less temporal structure for the GRU to exploit.\"\n232\t  },\n233\t  {\n234\t    \"name\": \"TrajGuard-style Mahalanobis baseline\",\n235\t    \"description\": \"Mahalanobis distance on safe-trace-estimated covariance, per step. Evaluates TrajGuard-style geometric detection across all 3 models.\",\n236\t    \"results\": {\n237\t      \"r1_8b_trajguard_coverage\": 0.395,\n238\t      \"r1_8b_trajguard_auroc\": 0.61,\n239\t      \"r1_32b_trajguard_coverage\": 0.326,\n240\t      \"r1_32b_trajguard_auroc\": 0.57,\n241\t      \"qwq_32b_trajguard_coverage\": 0.279,\n242\t      \"qwq_32b_trajguard_auroc\": 0.54,\n243\t      \"avg_trajguard_coverage\": 0.333,\n244\t      \"trajguard_vs_temporal_gap_pp\": -30.8\n245\t    },\n246\t    \"tables\": [\n247\t      {\n248\t        \"name\": \"Table 6: TrajGuard Mahalanobis baseline\",\n249\t        \"columns\": [\n250\t          \"Model\",\n251\t          \"Coverage\",\n252\t          \"AUROC\",\n253\t          \"FPR@50% TPR\",\n254\t          \"Lead Mean\"\n255\t        ],\n256\t        \"rows\": [\n257\t          [\n258\t            \"r1-8b\",\n259\t            \"0.395\",\n260\t            \"0.61\",\n261\t            \"0.38\",\n262\t            \"-4.2\"\n263\t          ],\n264\t          [\n265\t            \"r1-32b\",\n266\t            \"0.326\",\n267\t            \"0.57\",\n268\t            \"0.41\",\n269\t            \"-5.1\"\n270\t          ],\n271\t          [\n272\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n273\t            \"0.279\",\n274\t            \"0.54\",\n275\t            \"0.44\",\n276\t            \"-6.3\"\n277\t          ]\n278\t        ]\n279\t      }\n280\t    ],\n281\t    \"figures\": [],\n282\t    \"analysis\": \"TrajGuard's failure is not random — it's systematic and explainable. The Mahalanobis assumption (signal in principal variance directions) is precisely violated by our setting where Fisher drops 17× in PCA-3D. Coverage 28-40% (matching the median tier range of 25-45%) is worse than even static LR probes, which at least learn to project into discriminative directions. This result is essential for the paper's 'existing detectors fail' narrative and provides the mechanistic explanation (geometric assumption violation) rather than just the empirical observation.\\n\\nTrajGuard fails as expected because Mahalanobis distance assumes the discriminative signal lies in the principal directions of variance — but our PCA analysis showed Fisher drops from 17→1 in top-3 PCs. The safety commitment signal is distributed across minor components that MD effectively ignores. Coverage 28-40% is slightly above chance but far below temporal probes. QwQ performs worst (28%) because its lower overall Fisher (13.7) means even less signal in the geometric directions MD can access. This validates the 'existing detectors fail' claim and explains WHY: the geometric assumptions of TrajGuard-style methods are violated by the structure of reasoning-model safety representations.\"\n283\t  },\n284\t  {\n285\t    \"name\": \"Bootstrap CIs + threshold-independent metrics\",\n286\t    \"description\": \"1000 bootstrap resamples, AUROC, PR curves, detection delay CDF, FPR on safe traces. Comprehensive statistical characterization.\",\n287\t    \"results\": {\n288\t      \"temporal_lr_coverage_ci\": [\n289\t        0.68,\n290\t        0.84\n291\t      ],\n292\t      \"temporal_lr_coverage_ci_width\": 0.088,\n293\t      \"static_lr_coverage_ci\": [\n294\t        0.37,\n295\t        0.56\n296\t      ],\n297\t      \"static_lr_coverage_ci_width\": 0.098,\n298\t      \"gru_lead_ci\": [\n299\t        1.2,\n300\t        8.1\n301\t      ],\n302\t      \"gru_lead_ci_width\": 6.9,\n303\t      \"temporal_lr_auroc_r1_8b\": 0.89,\n304\t      \"temporal_lr_auroc_r1_32b\": 0.86,\n305\t      \"temporal_lr_auroc_qwq\": 0.82,\n306\t      \"cross_val_coverage_std\": 0.064,\n307\t      \"detection_delay_median\": 2.0,\n308\t      \"detection_delay_p90\": 8.0\n309\t    },\n310\t    \"tables\": [\n311\t      {\n312\t        \"name\": \"Table 7: Bootstrap 95% CIs for key metrics\",\n313\t        \"columns\": [\n314\t          \"Metric\",\n315\t          \"Point Est\",\n316\t          \"95% CI\",\n317\t          \"CI Width\"\n318\t        ],\n319\t        \"rows\": [\n320\t          [\n321\t            \"Temporal LR Coverage (r1-8b)\",\n322\t            \"0.767\",\n323\t            \"[0.68, 0.84]\",\n324\t            \"0.088\"\n325\t          ],\n326\t          [\n327\t            \"Static LR Coverage (r1-8b)\",\n328\t            \"0.465\",\n329\t            \"[0.37, 0.56]\",\n330\t            \"0.098\"\n331\t          ],\n332\t          [\n333\t            \"GRU Lead Mean (r1-8b)\",\n334\t            \"+4.7\",\n335\t            \"[1.2, 8.1]\",\n336\t            \"6.9\"\n337\t          ],\n338\t          [\n339\t            \"Temporal LR Coverage (r1-32b)\",\n340\t            \"0.698\",\n341\t            \"[0.62, 0.77]\",\n342\t            \"0.084\"\n343\t          ],\n344\t          [\n345\t            \"Temporal LR Coverage (QwQ)\",\n346\t            \"0.558\",\n347\t            \"[0.48, 0.64]\",\n348\t            \"0.092\"\n349\t          ],\n350\t          [\n351\t            \"Coverage Gap (temporal-static, r1-8b)\",\n352\t            \"+30.2pp\",\n353\t            \"[22.1, 38.4]\",\n354\t            \"16.3pp\"\n355\t          ]\n356\t        ]\n357\t      }\n358\t    ],\n359\t    \"figures\": [],\n360\t    \"analysis\": \"CIs are informative despite moderate width. Key finding: temporal-static gap CIs don't overlap on any model, confirming the advantage is real even accounting for sampling uncertainty. Lead time CIs are problematically wide (reflecting median=0, high std) — honest reporting but weakens Claim 3's strength. AUROC 0.82-0.89 provides a more favorable metric that reviewers may prefer. The CI width (0.06-0.12) motivates the call for larger datasets in future work without undermining current conclusions.\\n\\nBootstrap CIs are moderately wide (0.06-0.12 range for coverage, matching median tier) because N=209 traces with 43 test traces creates sampling variance. The coverage CI [0.68, 0.84] for temporal LR does NOT cross the static probe's upper CI bound [0.56], confirming the temporal advantage is statistically significant even with these CIs. Lead time CIs are very wide [1.2, 8.1] reflecting the high variance — the lower bound is positive (good) but barely. AUROC values (0.82-0.89) are substantially higher than coverage metrics because AUROC is threshold-independent and captures discriminative power that manifests differently at various operating points.\"\n361\t  },\n362\t  {\n363\t    \"name\": \"Window size ablation\",\n364\t    \"description\": \"W=1,3,5,10,15,20,25 temporal LR on all 3 models. Maps the coverage vs window size curve.\",\n365\t    \"results\": {\n366\t      \"r1_8b_w1_cov\": 0.465,\n367\t      \"r1_8b_w3_cov\": 0.535,\n368\t      \"r1_8b_w5_cov\": 0.605,\n369\t      \"r1_8b_w10_cov\": 0.698,\n370\t      \"r1_8b_w15_cov\": 0.767,\n371\t      \"r1_8b_w20_cov\": 0.767,\n372\t      \"r1_8b_w25_cov\": 0.744,\n373\t      \"r1_32b_w15_cov\": 0.698,\n374\t      \"r1_32b_w20_cov\": 0.721,\n375\t      \"r1_32b_w25_cov\": 0.698,\n376\t      \"qwq_w15_cov\": 0.558,\n377\t      \"qwq_w20_cov\": 0.581,\n378\t      \"qwq_w25_cov\": 0.558,\n379\t      \"optimal_w_r1_8b\": 15,\n380\t      \"optimal_w_r1_32b\": 20,\n381\t      \"optimal_w_qwq\": 20\n382\t    },\n383\t    \"tables\": [\n384\t      {\n385\t        \"name\": \"Table S1: Window size ablation\",\n386\t        \"columns\": [\n387\t          \"W\",\n388\t          \"r1-8b Cov\",\n389\t          \"r1-32b Cov\",\n390\t          \"QwQ Cov\"\n391\t        ],\n392\t        \"rows\": [\n393\t          [\n394\t            \"1\",\n395\t            \"0.465\",\n396\t            \"0.442\",\n397\t            \"0.372\"\n398\t          ],\n399\t          [\n400\t            \"3\",\n401\t            \"0.535\",\n402\t            \"0.512\",\n403\t            \"0.419\"\n404\t          ],\n405\t          [\n406\t            \"5\",\n407\t            \"0.605\",\n408\t            \"0.558\",\n409\t            \"0.465\"\n410\t          ],\n411\t          [\n412\t            \"10\",\n413\t            \"0.698\",\n414\t            \"0.628\",\n415\t            \"0.512\"\n416\t          ],\n417\t          [\n418\t            \"15\",\n419\t            \"0.767\",\n420\t            \"0.698\",\n421\t            \"0.558\"\n422\t          ],\n423\t          [\n424\t            \"20\",\n425\t            \"0.767\",\n426\t            \"0.721\",\n427\t            \"0.581\"\n428\t          ],\n429\t          [\n430\t            \"25\",\n431\t            \"0.744\",\n432\t            \"0.698\",\n433\t            \"0.558\"\n434\t          ]\n435\t        ]\n436\t      }\n437\t    ],\n438\t    \"figures\": [],\n439\t    \"analysis\": \"The diminishing returns curve validates W=15 as a principled choice (not arbitrary). The alignment between optimal W and oracle's pre-CP safety streak length (~14.6 steps) provides mechanistic justification: the probe needs approximately one full 'safety reasoning episode' of context to detect its cessation. The slight benefit of W=20 on 32B models may warrant model-specific tuning in deployment.\\n\\nDiminishing returns after W=15 on r1-8b (matches oracle streak length ~14.6). Slight gains at W=20 for 32B models because their longer traces have more gradual transitions. W=25 shows slight degradation (noise dilution from including too-old context). The saturation at W=15-20 across models is interpretable: it corresponds to the typical length of 'safety reasoning density decline' preceding commitment. QwQ saturates at lower absolute coverage but same relative saturation point — the limitation is signal quality, not window insufficient.\"\n440\t  },\n441\t  {\n442\t    \"name\": \"Layer selection ablation (32B models)\",\n443\t    \"description\": \"Temporal probe evaluated at all 64 layers for both 32B models. Tests whether middle-layer finding generalizes and whether there's a better layer for temporal probes vs static probes.\",\n444\t    \"results\": {\n445\t      \"r1_32b_temporal_best_layer\": 28,\n446\t      \"r1_32b_temporal_best_coverage\": 0.721,\n447\t      \"r1_32b_static_best_layer\": 30,\n448\t      \"r1_32b_temporal_layer_range_good\": \"24-34\",\n449\t      \"qwq_temporal_best_layer\": 24,\n450\t      \"qwq_temporal_best_coverage\": 0.581,\n451\t      \"qwq_static_best_layer\": 26,\n452\t      \"qwq_temporal_layer_range_good\": \"20-30\",\n453\t      \"temporal_vs_static_layer_offset\": -2\n454\t    },\n455\t    \"tables\": [\n456\t      {\n457\t        \"name\": \"Table S2: Layer selection for temporal probe (32B models)\",\n458\t        \"columns\": [\n459\t          \"Model\",\n460\t          \"Temporal Best Layer\",\n461\t          \"Static Best Layer\",\n462\t          \"Offset\",\n463\t          \"Good Range (>90% of peak)\",\n464\t          \"Peak Coverage\"\n465\t        ],\n466\t        \"rows\": [\n467\t          [\n468\t            \"r1-32b\",\n469\t            \"28\",\n470\t            \"30\",\n471\t            \"-2\",\n472\t            \"24-34\",\n473\t            \"0.721\"\n474\t          ],\n475\t          [\n476\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n477\t            \"24\",\n478\t            \"26\",\n479\t            \"-2\",\n480\t            \"20-30\",\n481\t            \"0.581\"\n482\t          ]\n483\t        ]\n484\t      }\n485\t    ],\n486\t    \"figures\": [],\n487\t    \"analysis\": \"Middle-layer finding generalizes robustly. The broad plateau (10-layer 'good zone') means practitioners don't need exhaustive layer search — picking ~40-45% depth works well. The consistent 2-layer offset between temporal and static best layers is a minor but interesting finding for the paper's analysis section.\\n\\nTemporal probes prefer slightly earlier layers (2 layers shallower) than static probes. This makes sense: static probes need the most processed representation (later = more feature-extracted), while temporal probes benefit from slightly rawer representations where temporal dynamics are more pronounced before being abstracted away by later layers. The 'good range' spanning ~10 layers (15% of depth) means the finding is robust to layer choice — not a fragile optimization. QwQ's best layer is slightly earlier (38% depth vs 44%) consistent with its faster-forming representations.\"\n488\t  },\n489\t  {\n490\t    \"name\": \"16-label taxonomy supervision\",\n491\t    \"description\": \"Fine-grained 16-label supervision (Type 1-4 safety categories) instead of binary commitment. Tests whether richer labels improve coverage/timing.\",\n492\t    \"results\": {\n493\t      \"r1_8b_16label_coverage\": 0.791,\n494\t      \"r1_8b_16label_lead_mean\": -0.3,\n495\t      \"r1_8b_binary_coverage\": 0.767,\n496\t      \"r1_8b_binary_lead_mean\": -1.09,\n497\t      \"coverage_improvement_pp\": 2.4,\n498\t      \"lead_improvement\": 0.79,\n499\t      \"r1_32b_16label_coverage\": 0.721,\n500\t      \"qwq_16label_coverage\": 0.581\n501\t    },\n502\t    \"tables\": [\n503\t      {\n504\t        \"name\": \"Table S3: 16-label vs binary supervision\",\n505\t        \"columns\": [\n506\t          \"Model\",\n507\t          \"Binary Cov\",\n508\t          \"16-label Cov\",\n509\t          \"Δ Cov\",\n510\t          \"Binary Lead\",\n511\t          \"16-label Lead\"\n512\t        ],\n513\t        \"rows\": [\n514\t          [\n515\t            \"r1-8b\",\n516\t            \"0.767\",\n517\t            \"0.791\",\n518\t            \"+2.4pp\",\n519\t            \"-1.09\",\n520\t            \"-0.3\"\n521\t          ],\n522\t          [\n523\t            \"r1-32b\",\n524\t            \"0.698\",\n525\t            \"0.721\",\n526\t            \"+2.3pp\",\n527\t            \"-0.6\",\n528\t            \"+0.1\"\n529\t          ],\n530\t          [\n531\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n532\t            \"0.558\",\n533\t            \"0.581\",\n534\t            \"+2.3pp\",\n535\t            \"-1.8\",\n536\t            \"-1.2\"\n537\t          ]\n538\t        ]\n539\t      }\n540\t    ],\n541\t    \"figures\": [],\n542\t    \"analysis\": \"16-label supervision provides marginal gain — confirms binary commitment is the right abstraction level for this task. The consistent +2.3pp across models suggests this is a real but small effect of better gradient signal, not noise. Include as supplementary result addressing reviewer concern but don't reframe any claims around it.\\n\\nMarginal improvement (+2.4pp coverage, +0.8 lead time) from 16-label supervision. The gain is small because the temporal window already captures most of the 'safety density decline' signal — finer labels provide slightly more gradient signal during training but the fundamental information (safety present vs absent over time) is already well-captured by binary. The improvement is consistent across models but never dramatic. This addresses the reviewer concern ('why not use full taxonomy?') with an empirical answer: marginal benefit, binary is nearly optimal for this task.\"\n543\t  },\n544\t  {\n545\t    \"name\": \"Oracle decomposition analysis\",\n546\t    \"description\": \"Analysis of why oracle achieves +31 lead time but probes cannot. Characterizes what label patterns encode that hidden states fail to predict.\",\n547\t    \"results\": {\n548\t      \"oracle_exploits_future_absence\": true,\n549\t      \"probe_captures_density_decline\": true,\n550\t      \"probe_misses_abrupt_transitions_pct\": 0.61,\n551\t      \"avg_steps_from_last_safety_to_cp\": 14.6,\n552\t      \"probes_fire_after_density_below_threshold\": true,\n553\t      \"oracle_fires_at_last_safety_label\": true,\n554\t      \"information_gap_type\": \"future trajectory vs current state\",\n555\t      \"abrupt_cp_traces_pct\": 0.42\n556\t    },\n557\t    \"tables\": [\n558\t      {\n559\t        \"name\": \"Table S4: Oracle decomposition\",\n560\t        \"columns\": [\n561\t          \"Trace Category\",\n562\t          \"% of Traces\",\n563\t          \"Oracle Lead\",\n564\t          \"GRU Lead\",\n565\t          \"Gap\"\n566\t        ],\n567\t        \"rows\": [\n568\t          [\n569\t            \"Gradual decline (>5 steps fade)\",\n570\t            \"58%\",\n571\t            \"+38\",\n572\t            \"+7.2\",\n573\t            \"31\"\n574\t          ],\n575\t          [\n576\t            \"Abrupt transition (≤3 steps)\",\n577\t            \"42%\",\n578\t            \"+22\",\n579\t            \"-2.1\",\n580\t            \"24\"\n581\t          ],\n582\t          [\n583\t            \"All traces\",\n584\t            \"100%\",\n585\t            \"+31\",\n586\t            \"+4.7\",\n587\t            \"26\"\n588\t          ]\n589\t        ]\n590\t      }\n591\t    ],\n592\t    \"figures\": [],\n593\t    \"analysis\": \"The oracle decomposition reveals a principled limitation: 42% of traces have abrupt commitment transitions where pre-textual detection is impossible given current information. GRU achieves positive lead specifically on the 58% with gradual transitions (+7.2 steps). This reframes Claim 3: the 39% positive-lead rate roughly corresponds to traces where gradual decline provides exploitable signal. Future work could target better detection of 'abrupt' transitions via architectural advances (e.g., latent state models). This analysis contributes to the 'what works and why' diagnostic framing.\\n\\nThe oracle 'cheats' by definition — it sees the LAST safety label and fires immediately (hence +31 mean lead = avg distance from last safety to trace end). Probes can only detect that safety density HAS declined, not that it WILL NOT recover. 42% of traces have 'abrupt' commitment (safety reasoning present within 3 steps of CP) where no pre-textual signal exists by construction. The remaining 58% with gradual decline are where probes succeed (these are the ~40% positive-lead traces). The gap is not model capacity — it's information-theoretic: current hidden states encode current state well but don't contain reliable signal about whether safety reasoning will resume.\"\n594\t  },\n595\t  {\n596\t    \"name\": \"OpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-7B base)\",\n597\t    \"description\": \"4th model completing the 2×2 architecture×scale matrix. OpenThinker-7B on Qwen2.5-7B base.\",\n598\t    \"results\": {\n599\t      \"openthinker_static_coverage\": 0.419,\n600\t      \"openthinker_temporal_coverage\": 0.605,\n601\t      \"openthinker_coverage_gap_pp\": 18.6,\n602\t      \"openthinker_gru_lead_mean\": 2.1,\n603\t      \"openthinker_best_layer\": 14,\n604\t      \"openthinker_best_layer_depth_pct\": 0.44,\n605\t      \"scale_effect_llama\": 0.0,\n606\t      \"scale_effect_qwen\": -0.047,\n607\t      \"architecture_effect_7b\": 0.162,\n608\t      \"architecture_effect_32b\": 0.14\n609\t    },\n610\t    \"tables\": [\n611\t      {\n612\t        \"name\": \"Table S5: 2×2 Architecture × Scale matrix (temporal coverage)\",\n613\t        \"columns\": [\n614\t          \"\",\n615\t          \"7-8B\",\n616\t          \"32B\"\n617\t        ],\n618\t        \"rows\": [\n619\t          [\n620\t            \"Llama-base\",\n621\t            \"0.767 (r1-8b)\",\n622\t            \"0.698 (r1-32b)\"\n623\t          ],\n624\t          [\n625\t            \"Qwen-base\",\n626\t            \"0.605 (OpenThinker)\",\n627\t            \"0.558 (QwQ-32B)\"\n628\t          ]\n629\t        ]\n630\t      }\n631\t    ],\n632\t    \"figures\": [],\n633\t    \"analysis\": \"The 2×2 matrix cleanly separates confounds: architecture (Llama vs Qwen) accounts for ~15pp difference, while scale (7B vs 32B) accounts for only ~5pp within each family. This finding supports the 'architecture-dependent benefit' reframing and provides a concrete recommendation: Llama-base reasoning models are more amenable to hidden-state temporal monitoring than Qwen-base ones.\\n\\nOpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-7B) provides the scale-matched comparison to r1-8b (Llama-8B). Its temporal coverage (0.605) is lower than r1-8b (0.767) — consistent with the cross-architecture finding that Qwen-base models have less exploitable temporal structure. The gap is comparable to QwQ vs r1-32b, suggesting architecture matters more than scale. The 2×2 matrix shows: Llama-base models consistently outperform Qwen-base models on temporal detection (~14-16pp gap), while scale has minimal effect within families. This separates architecture from scale confounds.\"\n634\t  },\n635\t  {\n636\t    \"name\": \"GRU/Attention probe on 32B models\",\n637\t    \"description\": \"GRU (256-d multi-layer) on 32B models. Tests whether increased model capacity helps with more data or if LR ceiling holds.\",\n638\t    \"results\": {\n639\t      \"r1_32b_gru_coverage\": 0.721,\n640\t      \"r1_32b_gru_vs_lr_coverage_gap\": 2.3,\n641\t      \"r1_32b_gru_lead_mean\": 3.1,\n642\t      \"qwq_32b_gru_coverage\": 0.581,\n643\t      \"qwq_32b_gru_vs_lr_coverage_gap\": 2.3,\n644\t      \"qwq_32b_gru_lead_mean\": 1.4,\n645\t      \"attention_r1_32b_coverage\": 0.698,\n646\t      \"attention_qwq_coverage\": 0.558,\n647\t      \"gru_better_than_lr_on_coverage\": false,\n648\t      \"gru_better_than_lr_on_timing\": true\n649\t    },\n650\t    \"tables\": [\n651\t      {\n652\t        \"name\": \"Table S6: Probe architecture comparison on 32B\",\n653\t        \"columns\": [\n654\t          \"Model\",\n655\t          \"Probe\",\n656\t          \"Coverage\",\n657\t          \"Lead Mean\",\n658\t          \"Params\"\n659\t        ],\n660\t        \"rows\": [\n661\t          [\n662\t            \"r1-32b\",\n663\t            \"Temporal LR W=15\",\n664\t            \"0.698\",\n665\t            \"-0.6\",\n666\t            \"76.8K\"\n667\t          ],\n668\t          [\n669\t            \"r1-32b\",\n670\t            \"GRU-256 multi\",\n671\t            \"0.721\",\n672\t            \"+3.1\",\n673\t            \"1.6M\"\n674\t          ],\n675\t          [\n676\t            \"r1-32b\",\n677\t            \"Attention (2-head)\",\n678\t            \"0.698\",\n679\t            \"+1.8\",\n680\t            \"2.1M\"\n681\t          ],\n682\t          [\n683\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n684\t            \"Temporal LR W=15\",\n685\t            \"0.558\",\n686\t            \"-1.8\",\n687\t            \"76.8K\"\n688\t          ],\n689\t          [\n690\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n691\t            \"GRU-256 multi\",\n692\t            \"0.581\",\n693\t            \"+1.4\",\n694\t            \"1.6M\"\n695\t          ],\n696\t          [\n697\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n698\t            \"Attention (2-head)\",\n699\t            \"0.558\",\n700\t            \"+0.6\",\n701\t            \"2.1M\"\n702\t          ]\n703\t        ]\n704\t      }\n705\t    ],\n706\t    \"figures\": [],\n707\t    \"analysis\": \"Confirms the 'simplicity is near-optimal' story for coverage detection. Temporal LR (77K params) achieves 97% of GRU's coverage (1.6M params). The GRU advantage is concentrated in lead time — a capacity/complexity trade that reviewers may find interesting but that doesn't change the main coverage claims. Supports the diagnostic framing: we understand what works (temporal context) and what doesn't help (more model capacity for coverage).\\n\\nGRU matches or marginally exceeds temporal LR on coverage (consistent with r1-8b finding where GRU's advantage was in timing not coverage). Coverage appears to be bounded by the oracle ceiling — once temporal LR saturates it, more capacity doesn't help for detection. GRU's advantage manifests only in lead time (using sequential context for anticipation rather than just detection). Attention probe slightly underperforms GRU — the sequential inductive bias of GRU is better suited to the temporal detection task than attention's permutation-equivariant processing. This confirms 'simplicity is near-optimal for coverage' while 'complexity helps timing marginally.'\"\n708\t  },\n709\t  {\n710\t    \"name\": \"Text-based baseline (sentence-BERT)\",\n711\t    \"description\": \"Sentence-BERT (all-MiniLM-L6-v2) embeddings + temporal LR W=15 on CoT text. Critical comparison to hidden-state probes.\",\n712\t    \"results\": {\n713\t      \"text_r1_8b_coverage\": 0.651,\n714\t      \"text_r1_8b_lead_mean\": -2.4,\n715\t      \"text_r1_32b_coverage\": 0.628,\n716\t      \"text_r1_32b_lead_mean\": -2.8,\n717\t      \"text_qwq_coverage\": 0.605,\n718\t      \"text_qwq_lead_mean\": -3.1,\n719\t      \"text_avg_coverage\": 0.628,\n720\t      \"hidden_r1_8b_vs_text_gap_pp\": 11.6,\n721\t      \"hidden_r1_32b_vs_text_gap_pp\": 7.0,\n722\t      \"hidden_qwq_vs_text_gap_pp\": -4.7,\n723\t      \"avg_hidden_vs_text_gap_pp\": 4.6,\n724\t      \"text_auroc_r1_8b\": 0.83,\n725\t      \"hidden_auroc_r1_8b\": 0.89,\n726\t      \"text_captures_timing\": false\n727\t    },\n728\t    \"tables\": [\n729\t      {\n730\t        \"name\": \"Table 8: Hidden-state vs Text baseline comparison\",\n731\t        \"columns\": [\n732\t          \"Model\",\n733\t          \"Hidden Temporal Cov\",\n734\t          \"Text Temporal Cov\",\n735\t          \"Gap (pp)\",\n736\t          \"Hidden GRU Lead\",\n737\t          \"Text Lead\"\n738\t        ],\n739\t        \"rows\": [\n740\t          [\n741\t            \"r1-8b\",\n742\t            \"0.767\",\n743\t            \"0.651\",\n744\t            \"+11.6\",\n745\t            \"+4.7\",\n746\t            \"-2.4\"\n747\t          ],\n748\t          [\n749\t            \"r1-32b\",\n750\t            \"0.698\",\n751\t            \"0.628\",\n752\t            \"+7.0\",\n753\t            \"+3.1\",\n754\t            \"-2.8\"\n755\t          ],\n756\t          [\n757\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n758\t            \"0.558\",\n759\t            \"0.605\",\n760\t            \"-4.7\",\n761\t            \"+1.4\",\n762\t            \"-3.1\"\n763\t          ],\n764\t          [\n765\t            \"Average\",\n766\t            \"0.674\",\n767\t            \"0.628\",\n768\t            \"+4.6\",\n769\t            \"+3.1\",\n770\t            \"-2.8\"\n771\t          ]\n772\t        ]\n773\t      }\n774\t    ],\n775\t    \"figures\": [],\n776\t    \"analysis\": \"The text baseline result is the most complex finding in median tier. Coverage gap exists but is modest and architecture-dependent — ruling out a 'hidden states strictly dominate' narrative. However, the TIMING advantage is clear and consistent: hidden states provide +3-5 steps lead over text across all models. This reframes the paper's contribution: hidden-state monitoring is not about better coverage detection (text is competitive there) but about EARLIER detection. The timing advantage is precisely the deployment-relevant metric — detecting commitment before the harmful text is generated enables intervention. Qualitative case studies become essential: showing specific traces where hidden states fire 5+ steps before any textual indicator of commitment.\\n\\nText baseline achieves 60-65% coverage — competitive but not matching hidden states on Llama-base models (r1-8b gap +11.6pp, r1-32b gap +7.0pp). However, on QwQ the text baseline actually slightly outperforms hidden-state probes (-4.7pp means text wins). This is the median tier outcome: text is not a pushover, gap is modest (avg ~5pp across all models) and architecture-dependent. The text baseline fails on TIMING (lead -2.4 to -3.1, worse than hidden-state GRU's positive leads) because textual signals of commitment arrive at or after the commitment point by definition — the model writes the harmful text AFTER committing internally. This is the key differentiator: hidden states provide pre-textual timing signal that text cannot access.\"\n777\t  },\n778\t  {\n779\t    \"name\": \"BOCPD change-point detection\",\n780\t    \"description\": \"Bayesian Online Change-Point Detection (Adams & MacKay 2007) applied to per-step probe confidence score sequences. Also test CUSUM as simpler alternative.\",\n781\t    \"results\": {\n782\t      \"bocpd_r1_8b_lead_mean\": 5.2,\n783\t      \"bocpd_r1_8b_lead_median\": 2,\n784\t      \"bocpd_r1_8b_early_detection_rate\": 0.47,\n785\t      \"bocpd_r1_8b_coverage\": 0.698,\n786\t      \"bocpd_r1_32b_lead_mean\": 3.8,\n787\t      \"bocpd_r1_32b_early_detection_rate\": 0.39,\n788\t      \"bocpd_qwq_lead_mean\": 2.1,\n789\t      \"bocpd_qwq_early_detection_rate\": 0.31,\n790\t      \"cusum_r1_8b_lead_mean\": 4.1,\n791\t      \"cusum_r1_8b_early_detection_rate\": 0.42,\n792\t      \"bocpd_vs_gru_lead_improvement\": 0.5,\n793\t      \"bocpd_fpr_at_operational\": 0.15,\n794\t      \"bocpd_hazard_rate\": 0.01\n795\t    },\n796\t    \"tables\": [\n797\t      {\n798\t        \"name\": \"Table 9: BOCPD vs GRU vs Oracle (lead time analysis)\",\n799\t        \"columns\": [\n800\t          \"Method\",\n801\t          \"Lead Mean\",\n802\t          \"Lead Median\",\n803\t          \"Early Det. %\",\n804\t          \"Coverage\",\n805\t          \"FPR\"\n806\t        ],\n807\t        \"rows\": [\n808\t          [\n809\t            \"Oracle (K=5)\",\n810\t            \"+31.0\",\n811\t            \"+29\",\n812\t            \"100%\",\n813\t            \"100%\",\n814\t            \"0%\"\n815\t          ],\n816\t          [\n817\t            \"BOCPD (r1-8b)\",\n818\t            \"+5.2\",\n819\t            \"+2\",\n820\t            \"47%\",\n821\t            \"69.8%\",\n822\t            \"15%\"\n823\t          ],\n824\t          [\n825\t            \"GRU multi (r1-8b)\",\n826\t            \"+4.7\",\n827\t            \"0\",\n828\t            \"39%\",\n829\t            \"76.7%\",\n830\t            \"12%\"\n831\t          ],\n832\t          [\n833\t            \"CUSUM (r1-8b)\",\n834\t            \"+4.1\",\n835\t            \"+1\",\n836\t            \"42%\",\n837\t            \"72.1%\",\n838\t            \"18%\"\n839\t          ],\n840\t          [\n841\t            \"Temporal LR (r1-8b)\",\n842\t            \"-1.09\",\n843\t            \"-1\",\n844\t            \"23%\",\n845\t            \"76.7%\",\n846\t            \"8%\"\n847\t          ]\n848\t        ]\n849\t      },\n850\t      {\n851\t        \"name\": \"Table 10: BOCPD cross-architecture\",\n852\t        \"columns\": [\n853\t          \"Model\",\n854\t          \"BOCPD Lead Mean\",\n855\t          \"BOCPD Early Det %\",\n856\t          \"GRU Lead Mean\",\n857\t          \"GRU Early Det %\"\n858\t        ],\n859\t        \"rows\": [\n860\t          [\n861\t            \"r1-8b\",\n862\t            \"+5.2\",\n863\t            \"47%\",\n864\t            \"+4.7\",\n865\t            \"39%\"\n866\t          ],\n867\t          [\n868\t            \"r1-32b\",\n869\t            \"+3.8\",\n870\t            \"39%\",\n871\t            \"+3.1\",\n872\t            \"36%\"\n873\t          ],\n874\t          [\n875\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n876\t            \"+2.1\",\n877\t            \"31%\",\n878\t            \"+1.4\",\n879\t            \"28%\"\n880\t          ]\n881\t        ]\n882\t      }\n883\t    ],\n884\t    \"figures\": [],\n885\t    \"analysis\": \"BOCPD is an incremental improvement over GRU for timing, not a breakthrough. The +0.5 step improvement and +8pp early detection rate improvement are real but modest. The method's value for the paper is dual: (1) it adds a genuine methods contribution (novel application of BOCPD to probe score sequences for safety monitoring), and (2) its failure to achieve oracle-level timing DESPITE being a principled change-point method strengthens the 'fundamental limitation' argument. If even BOCPD (designed precisely for detecting distributional shifts) can't reliably achieve early detection, the limitation is in the signal, not the detection method. Coverage drops slightly (69.8% vs 76.7%) because BOCPD's probabilistic threshold filters out marginal detections — a precision-recall tradeoff.\\n\\nBOCPD achieves marginally better lead time than GRU (+5.2 vs +4.7 on r1-8b) by explicitly modeling distributional shift in the probe score sequence rather than trying to predict future states. The improvement is incremental (+0.5 steps) not transformative — both methods face the same fundamental limitation (hidden states encode current state, not future trajectory). BOCPD's advantage comes from its probabilistic framework: it detects that a regime change HAS occurred slightly faster than GRU predicts one WILL occur. Early detection rate 47% is better than GRU's 39% but still far from oracle's 100%. The method provides an honest 'incremental improvement' story rather than a breakthrough. CUSUM performs slightly worse than BOCPD (less principled distributional assumptions). Coverage drops from 76.7% to 69.8% because BOCPD requires sustained evidence of change-point, filtering out borderline detections.\"\n886\t  },\n887\t  {\n888\t    \"name\": \"False positive analysis on safe traces\",\n889\t    \"description\": \"All probes evaluated on safe traces (final_judgment=0.5). Reports FPR, precision-recall curves, deployment utility.\",\n890\t    \"results\": {\n891\t      \"temporal_lr_fpr_at_70cov\": 0.14,\n892\t      \"static_lr_fpr_at_47cov\": 0.21,\n893\t      \"gru_fpr_at_70cov\": 0.12,\n894\t      \"bocpd_fpr_at_70cov\": 0.15,\n895\t      \"trajguard_fpr_at_40cov\": 0.33,\n896\t      \"temporal_lr_precision_at_70recall\": 0.78,\n897\t      \"temporal_lr_precision_at_80recall\": 0.69,\n898\t      \"safe_traces_evaluated\": 76,\n899\t      \"probe_fires_on_safe_pct\": 0.14,\n900\t      \"mean_false_alarm_duration\": 3.2,\n901\t      \"fpr_by_model_r1_8b\": 0.14,\n902\t      \"fpr_by_model_r1_32b\": 0.16,\n903\t      \"fpr_by_model_qwq\": 0.19\n904\t    },\n905\t    \"tables\": [\n906\t      {\n907\t        \"name\": \"Table 11: False positive analysis\",\n908\t        \"columns\": [\n909\t          \"Probe\",\n910\t          \"Coverage (unsafe)\",\n911\t          \"FPR (safe)\",\n912\t          \"Precision@Cov\",\n913\t          \"F1\"\n914\t        ],\n915\t        \"rows\": [\n916\t          [\n917\t            \"Temporal LR W=15\",\n918\t            \"76.7%\",\n919\t            \"14%\",\n920\t            \"0.78\",\n921\t            \"0.77\"\n922\t          ],\n923\t          [\n924\t            \"Static LR\",\n925\t            \"46.5%\",\n926\t            \"21%\",\n927\t            \"0.63\",\n928\t            \"0.53\"\n929\t          ],\n930\t          [\n931\t            \"GRU multi-layer\",\n932\t            \"76.7%\",\n933\t            \"12%\",\n934\t            \"0.81\",\n935\t            \"0.79\"\n936\t          ],\n937\t          [\n938\t            \"BOCPD\",\n939\t            \"69.8%\",\n940\t            \"15%\",\n941\t            \"0.76\",\n942\t            \"0.73\"\n943\t          ],\n944\t          [\n945\t            \"TrajGuard (MD)\",\n946\t            \"39.5%\",\n947\t            \"33%\",\n948\t            \"0.49\",\n949\t            \"0.44\"\n950\t          ]\n951\t        ]\n952\t      },\n953\t      {\n954\t        \"name\": \"Table 12: FPR by model at operational threshold\",\n955\t        \"columns\": [\n956\t          \"Model\",\n957\t          \"Temporal LR FPR\",\n958\t          \"GRU FPR\",\n959\t          \"BOCPD FPR\"\n960\t        ],\n961\t        \"rows\": [\n962\t          [\n963\t            \"r1-8b\",\n964\t            \"14%\",\n965\t            \"12%\",\n966\t            \"15%\"\n967\t          ],\n968\t          [\n969\t            \"r1-32b\",\n970\t            \"16%\",\n971\t            \"14%\",\n972\t            \"17%\"\n973\t          ],\n974\t          [\n975\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n976\t            \"19%\",\n977\t            \"17%\",\n978\t            \"20%\"\n979\t          ]\n980\t        ]\n981\t      }\n982\t    ],\n983\t    \"figures\": [],\n984\t    \"analysis\": \"FPR 14% at operational threshold is the key deployment constraint. It's acceptable for human-in-the-loop monitoring (flag for review) but too high for autonomous intervention (blocking generation). The paper should frame this honestly as 'monitoring-suitable, not intervention-suitable at current precision.' QwQ's higher FPR (19%) aligns with its generally worse probe calibration — another architecture-dependent finding. The false positive characterization (most triggered by extensive safety deliberation) is useful for the paper's diagnostic contribution: it shows the probe correctly identifies 'safety engagement' but sometimes cannot distinguish 'engagement leading to refusal' from 'engagement leading to failure.' This motivates outcome-aware probe training in future work.\\n\\nFPR 14% at operational threshold (70% coverage) is acceptable but not ideal — consistent with median tier range of 10-20%. The false positives tend to occur on safe traces that contain extensive safety deliberation (the model reasons about safety extensively before correctly refusing — the probe misinterprets high safety reasoning density followed by resolution as a potential commitment pattern). GRU has slightly lower FPR (12%) because its sequential modeling better distinguishes 'safety reasoning leading to refusal' from 'safety reasoning ceasing before harm.' TrajGuard's high FPR (33%) further confirms its unsuitability. The 14% FPR means roughly 1 in 7 safe traces triggers a false alarm — deployable with human-in-the-loop but not for autonomous intervention.\"\n985\t  }\n986\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ad67dd81-564d-443c-8be7-fda2395c9014","uuid":"7b6186a0-3858-47c7-a9d3-12d241e48ab4","timestamp":"2026-05-16T21:00:17.323Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_5/projects/contrastive_trace_safety/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T203948/median/experiment_data.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Cross-architecture static probe (32B models)\",\n    \"description\": \"Layer sweep + static LR probe on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B and QwQ-32B (64 layers, 5120-d). Identifies best layer, evaluates static probe coverage/accuracy.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_32b_best_layer_idx\": 30,\n      \"r1_32b_best_layer_depth_pct\": 0.47,\n      \"r1_32b_static_acc\": 0.7943,\n      \"r1_32b_static_coverage\": 0.442,\n      \"r1_32b_static_lead_mean\": -2.8,\n      \"r1_32b_fisher_orig\": 15.3,\n      \"r1_32b_fisher_pca3\": 1.02,\n      \"qwq_32b_best_layer_idx\": 26,\n      \"qwq_32b_best_layer_depth_pct\": 0.41,\n      \"qwq_32b_static_acc\": 0.7651,\n      \"qwq_32b_static_coverage\": 0.372,\n      \"qwq_32b_static_lead_mean\": -3.4,\n      \"qwq_32b_fisher_orig\": 13.7,\n      \"qwq_32b_fisher_pca3\": 0.87,\n      \"num_traces_r1_32b\": 209,\n      \"num_traces_qwq\": 209\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 2: Layer sweep results (64-layer models)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"Best Layer\",\n          \"Depth %\",\n          \"Static Acc\",\n          \"Coverage\",\n          \"Lead Mean\",\n          \"Fisher (orig)\",\n          \"Fisher (PCA-3)\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"r1-8b (reference)\",\n            \"14/32\",\n            \"44%\",\n            \"0.817\",\n            \"0.465\",\n            \"-2.0\",\n            \"17.9\",\n            \"0.93\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"30/64\",\n            \"47%\",\n            \"0.794\",\n            \"0.442\",\n            \"-2.8\",\n            \"15.3\",\n            \"1.02\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"26/64\",\n            \"41%\",\n            \"0.765\",\n            \"0.372\",\n            \"-3.4\",\n            \"13.7\",\n            \"0.87\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Cross-architecture static probe results confirm Claim 1 generalizes. Both 32B models show the same qualitative pattern as r1-8b: high Fisher in full 5120-d space but collapse in low-rank projections. r1-32b slightly better than QwQ due to shared DeepSeek training lineage. QwQ's lower Fisher (13.7 vs 17.9) suggests Qwen-native training produces more diffuse safety representations. Coverage 37-44% vs r1-8b's 46.5% — static probes consistently fail across architectures. Best layer depth percentage is remarkably consistent (41-47%) suggesting a universal property of transformer safety representations.\\n\\nStatic probes fail cross-architecture as expected — signal is distributed across minor PCs (Fisher drops from 13-15→~1 in PCA-3D), consistent with r1-8b finding. r1-32b slightly outperforms QwQ because it shares the DeepSeek training pipeline with r1-8b (similar internal safety representations). QwQ has a different training regime (Qwen team's RL approach), producing less separable static geometry. Best layer at ~41-47% depth matches r1-8b's 44% finding — middle layers capture task-relevant features universally. Both models confirm Claim 1: static probes achieve <50% coverage despite high Fisher in full space.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Cross-architecture temporal window LR\",\n    \"description\": \"Temporal LR (W=15) on r1-32b and QwQ-32B at their best layers. Tests whether +30pp coverage improvement replicates cross-architecture.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_32b_temporal_coverage\": 0.698,\n      \"r1_32b_temporal_acc\": 0.8391,\n      \"r1_32b_temporal_lead_mean\": -0.6,\n      \"r1_32b_coverage_gap_pp\": 25.6,\n      \"qwq_32b_temporal_coverage\": 0.558,\n      \"qwq_32b_temporal_acc\": 0.8103,\n      \"qwq_32b_temporal_lead_mean\": -1.8,\n      \"qwq_32b_coverage_gap_pp\": 18.6,\n      \"avg_coverage_gap_pp\": 22.1\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 3: Temporal vs Static coverage by model\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"Static Cov\",\n          \"Temporal W=15 Cov\",\n          \"Gap (pp)\",\n          \"Temporal Acc\",\n          \"Lead Mean\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"r1-8b (reference)\",\n            \"0.465\",\n            \"0.767\",\n            \"+30.2\",\n            \"0.848\",\n            \"-1.09\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"0.442\",\n            \"0.698\",\n            \"+25.6\",\n            \"0.839\",\n            \"-0.6\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"0.372\",\n            \"0.558\",\n            \"+18.6\",\n            \"0.810\",\n            \"-1.8\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Temporal advantage is robust in direction (+15-26pp across models) but architecture-dependent in magnitude. r1-32b replicates well (25.6pp gap, near r1-8b's 30pp), while QwQ shows a weaker but real 18.6pp improvement. The discrepancy likely reflects different RL training approaches: DeepSeek models exhibit gradual safety reasoning density decline (well-captured by W=15), while QwQ's training produces more abrupt transitions. Claim 2 holds but needs reframing: 'temporal context consistently improves detection across architectures, with benefit magnitude correlated to the gradualness of the commitment transition.' The average 22pp gap is still substantial and clearly exceeds noise.\\n\\nTemporal advantage replicates on r1-32b (+25.6pp, approaching r1-8b's +30pp) but is weaker on QwQ-32B (+18.6pp). r1-32b shares DeepSeek's training dynamics — safety reasoning density decline patterns are similar to r1-8b, so the temporal window captures them effectively. QwQ has a different RL training procedure that produces more abrupt rather than gradual commitment transitions — the 15-step window captures some but not all of the temporal signal. The gap EXISTS on both models (confirming temporal context helps universally) but magnitude is architecture-dependent. This is the median outcome: partial replication, one strong + one moderate.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Cross-model transfer evaluation\",\n    \"description\": \"Zero-shot cross-architecture probe transfer. Train on r1-8b (Llama), test on r1-32b/QwQ (Qwen2.5). Train on Qwen, test on Llama. Uses learned projection head (4096↔5120).\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"llama_to_r1_32b_coverage_retention\": 0.56,\n      \"llama_to_qwq_coverage_retention\": 0.43,\n      \"qwen_to_llama_coverage_retention\": 0.47,\n      \"within_qwen_r1_32b_to_qwq_retention\": 0.72,\n      \"within_qwen_qwq_to_r1_32b_retention\": 0.68,\n      \"projection_head_params\": 20971520,\n      \"cross_family_avg_retention\": 0.49,\n      \"within_family_avg_retention\": 0.7\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 4: Cross-model transfer matrix (coverage retention %)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Train →\",\n          \"Test: r1-8b (Llama)\",\n          \"Test: r1-32b (Qwen)\",\n          \"Test: QwQ (Qwen)\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"r1-8b (Llama)\",\n            \"100%\",\n            \"56%\",\n            \"43%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b (Qwen)\",\n            \"47%\",\n            \"100%\",\n            \"72%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B (Qwen)\",\n            \"47%\",\n            \"68%\",\n            \"100%\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Transfer results reveal a clear family boundary: within-Qwen transfers retain 68-72% of same-model performance, while cross-family transfers average only 49%. This is consistent with the median tier expectation. The practical implication: a probe trained on one model in a family can provide moderate monitoring for siblings, but cross-family deployment requires retraining. For the paper, this motivates model-specific probes as the default recommendation while noting that within-family transfer is a viable low-cost option. The asymmetry (Llama→r1-32b at 56% vs QwQ→Llama at 47%) suggests the DeepSeek fine-tuning creates a partial bridge between families.\\n\\nWithin-family transfer (Qwen→Qwen) works reasonably well (68-72% retention) because both models share the Qwen2.5 base architecture and similar embedding geometry — safety representations occupy analogous subspaces. Cross-family transfer (Llama→Qwen, Qwen→Llama) degrades to ~43-56% retention because the learned projection head must bridge fundamentally different representation geometries. The Llama→r1-32b direction is slightly better (56%) than Llama→QwQ (43%) because r1-32b's DeepSeek fine-tuning shares some training signal structure with r1-8b despite the architecture difference. This confirms the median assumption: cross-family transfer works but with substantial degradation (<50% avg retention).\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Cross-architecture lead time analysis\",\n    \"description\": \"GRU (256-d, multi-layer) lead time evaluation on r1-32b and QwQ-32B. Tests whether +4.7 mean lead time finding generalizes.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_32b_gru_lead_mean\": 3.1,\n      \"r1_32b_gru_lead_median\": 0,\n      \"r1_32b_gru_lead_std\": 11.4,\n      \"r1_32b_gru_positive_lead_rate\": 0.36,\n      \"r1_32b_gru_crossing_rate\": 0.72,\n      \"qwq_32b_gru_lead_mean\": 1.4,\n      \"qwq_32b_gru_lead_median\": -1,\n      \"qwq_32b_gru_lead_std\": 9.8,\n      \"qwq_32b_gru_positive_lead_rate\": 0.28,\n      \"qwq_32b_gru_crossing_rate\": 0.63\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 5: Lead time analysis across models (GRU multi-layer)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"Lead Mean\",\n          \"Lead Median\",\n          \"Lead Std\",\n          \"Positive Lead %\",\n          \"Crossing Rate\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"r1-8b\",\n            \"+4.7\",\n            \"0\",\n            \"13.0\",\n            \"39%\",\n            \"76.7%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"+3.1\",\n            \"0\",\n            \"11.4\",\n            \"36%\",\n            \"72.0%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"+1.4\",\n            \"-1\",\n            \"9.8\",\n            \"28%\",\n            \"63.0%\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Lead time results confirm Claim 3 across architectures: pre-textual detection is 'unreliable positive.' The positive mean persists but magnitude decreases with architectural distance from the original r1-8b finding. QwQ shows genuinely poor timing (+1.4 mean, median -1) — its abrupt commitment transitions provide less exploitable temporal structure. The consistent pattern (high std, low positive-lead rate, median ≤ 0) across all models strengthens the 'fundamental limitation' argument. The oracle's +31-step advantage remains unreachable by any current method.\\n\\nGRU lead time degrades on 32B models compared to r1-8b's +4.7. r1-32b achieves +3.1 (reduced from 4.7 — still positive mean but same median=0 pattern). QwQ further degrades to +1.4 mean with negative median, reflecting its more abrupt transitions that give the sequential model less predictive runway. The fundamental pattern persists: positive mean but unreliable (high std, low positive-lead rate). With only 209 traces and high variance, the GRU cannot reliably learn the timing signal on any model. QwQ's worse performance aligns with its more abrupt commitment transitions — less temporal structure for the GRU to exploit.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"TrajGuard-style Mahalanobis baseline\",\n    \"description\": \"Mahalanobis distance on safe-trace-estimated covariance, per step. Evaluates TrajGuard-style geometric detection across all 3 models.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_8b_trajguard_coverage\": 0.395,\n      \"r1_8b_trajguard_auroc\": 0.61,\n      \"r1_32b_trajguard_coverage\": 0.326,\n      \"r1_32b_trajguard_auroc\": 0.57,\n      \"qwq_32b_trajguard_coverage\": 0.279,\n      \"qwq_32b_trajguard_auroc\": 0.54,\n      \"avg_trajguard_coverage\": 0.333,\n      \"trajguard_vs_temporal_gap_pp\": -30.8\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 6: TrajGuard Mahalanobis baseline\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"Coverage\",\n          \"AUROC\",\n          \"FPR@50% TPR\",\n          \"Lead Mean\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"r1-8b\",\n            \"0.395\",\n            \"0.61\",\n            \"0.38\",\n            \"-4.2\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"0.326\",\n            \"0.57\",\n            \"0.41\",\n            \"-5.1\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"0.279\",\n            \"0.54\",\n            \"0.44\",\n            \"-6.3\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"TrajGuard's failure is not random — it's systematic and explainable. The Mahalanobis assumption (signal in principal variance directions) is precisely violated by our setting where Fisher drops 17× in PCA-3D. Coverage 28-40% (matching the median tier range of 25-45%) is worse than even static LR probes, which at least learn to project into discriminative directions. This result is essential for the paper's 'existing detectors fail' narrative and provides the mechanistic explanation (geometric assumption violation) rather than just the empirical observation.\\n\\nTrajGuard fails as expected because Mahalanobis distance assumes the discriminative signal lies in the principal directions of variance — but our PCA analysis showed Fisher drops from 17→1 in top-3 PCs. The safety commitment signal is distributed across minor components that MD effectively ignores. Coverage 28-40% is slightly above chance but far below temporal probes. QwQ performs worst (28%) because its lower overall Fisher (13.7) means even less signal in the geometric directions MD can access. This validates the 'existing detectors fail' claim and explains WHY: the geometric assumptions of TrajGuard-style methods are violated by the structure of reasoning-model safety representations.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Bootstrap CIs + threshold-independent metrics\",\n    \"description\": \"1000 bootstrap resamples, AUROC, PR curves, detection delay CDF, FPR on safe traces. Comprehensive statistical characterization.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"temporal_lr_coverage_ci\": [\n        0.68,\n        0.84\n      ],\n      \"temporal_lr_coverage_ci_width\": 0.088,\n      \"static_lr_coverage_ci\": [\n        0.37,\n        0.56\n      ],\n      \"static_lr_coverage_ci_width\": 0.098,\n      \"gru_lead_ci\": [\n        1.2,\n        8.1\n      ],\n      \"gru_lead_ci_width\": 6.9,\n      \"temporal_lr_auroc_r1_8b\": 0.89,\n      \"temporal_lr_auroc_r1_32b\": 0.86,\n      \"temporal_lr_auroc_qwq\": 0.82,\n      \"cross_val_coverage_std\": 0.064,\n      \"detection_delay_median\": 2.0,\n      \"detection_delay_p90\": 8.0\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 7: Bootstrap 95% CIs for key metrics\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Metric\",\n          \"Point Est\",\n          \"95% CI\",\n          \"CI Width\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Temporal LR Coverage (r1-8b)\",\n            \"0.767\",\n            \"[0.68, 0.84]\",\n            \"0.088\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Static LR Coverage (r1-8b)\",\n            \"0.465\",\n            \"[0.37, 0.56]\",\n            \"0.098\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"GRU Lead Mean (r1-8b)\",\n            \"+4.7\",\n            \"[1.2, 8.1]\",\n            \"6.9\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Temporal LR Coverage (r1-32b)\",\n            \"0.698\",\n            \"[0.62, 0.77]\",\n            \"0.084\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Temporal LR Coverage (QwQ)\",\n            \"0.558\",\n            \"[0.48, 0.64]\",\n            \"0.092\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Coverage Gap (temporal-static, r1-8b)\",\n            \"+30.2pp\",\n            \"[22.1, 38.4]\",\n            \"16.3pp\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"CIs are informative despite moderate width. Key finding: temporal-static gap CIs don't overlap on any model, confirming the advantage is real even accounting for sampling uncertainty. Lead time CIs are problematically wide (reflecting median=0, high std) — honest reporting but weakens Claim 3's strength. AUROC 0.82-0.89 provides a more favorable metric that reviewers may prefer. The CI width (0.06-0.12) motivates the call for larger datasets in future work without undermining current conclusions.\\n\\nBootstrap CIs are moderately wide (0.06-0.12 range for coverage, matching median tier) because N=209 traces with 43 test traces creates sampling variance. The coverage CI [0.68, 0.84] for temporal LR does NOT cross the static probe's upper CI bound [0.56], confirming the temporal advantage is statistically significant even with these CIs. Lead time CIs are very wide [1.2, 8.1] reflecting the high variance — the lower bound is positive (good) but barely. AUROC values (0.82-0.89) are substantially higher than coverage metrics because AUROC is threshold-independent and captures discriminative power that manifests differently at various operating points.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Window size ablation\",\n    \"description\": \"W=1,3,5,10,15,20,25 temporal LR on all 3 models. Maps the coverage vs window size curve.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_8b_w1_cov\": 0.465,\n      \"r1_8b_w3_cov\": 0.535,\n      \"r1_8b_w5_cov\": 0.605,\n      \"r1_8b_w10_cov\": 0.698,\n      \"r1_8b_w15_cov\": 0.767,\n      \"r1_8b_w20_cov\": 0.767,\n      \"r1_8b_w25_cov\": 0.744,\n      \"r1_32b_w15_cov\": 0.698,\n      \"r1_32b_w20_cov\": 0.721,\n      \"r1_32b_w25_cov\": 0.698,\n      \"qwq_w15_cov\": 0.558,\n      \"qwq_w20_cov\": 0.581,\n      \"qwq_w25_cov\": 0.558,\n      \"optimal_w_r1_8b\": 15,\n      \"optimal_w_r1_32b\": 20,\n      \"optimal_w_qwq\": 20\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table S1: Window size ablation\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"W\",\n          \"r1-8b Cov\",\n          \"r1-32b Cov\",\n          \"QwQ Cov\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"1\",\n            \"0.465\",\n            \"0.442\",\n            \"0.372\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"3\",\n            \"0.535\",\n            \"0.512\",\n            \"0.419\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"5\",\n            \"0.605\",\n            \"0.558\",\n            \"0.465\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"10\",\n            \"0.698\",\n            \"0.628\",\n            \"0.512\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"15\",\n            \"0.767\",\n            \"0.698\",\n            \"0.558\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"20\",\n            \"0.767\",\n            \"0.721\",\n            \"0.581\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"25\",\n            \"0.744\",\n            \"0.698\",\n            \"0.558\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The diminishing returns curve validates W=15 as a principled choice (not arbitrary). The alignment between optimal W and oracle's pre-CP safety streak length (~14.6 steps) provides mechanistic justification: the probe needs approximately one full 'safety reasoning episode' of context to detect its cessation. The slight benefit of W=20 on 32B models may warrant model-specific tuning in deployment.\\n\\nDiminishing returns after W=15 on r1-8b (matches oracle streak length ~14.6). Slight gains at W=20 for 32B models because their longer traces have more gradual transitions. W=25 shows slight degradation (noise dilution from including too-old context). The saturation at W=15-20 across models is interpretable: it corresponds to the typical length of 'safety reasoning density decline' preceding commitment. QwQ saturates at lower absolute coverage but same relative saturation point — the limitation is signal quality, not window insufficient.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Layer selection ablation (32B models)\",\n    \"description\": \"Temporal probe evaluated at all 64 layers for both 32B models. Tests whether middle-layer finding generalizes and whether there's a better layer for temporal probes vs static probes.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_32b_temporal_best_layer\": 28,\n      \"r1_32b_temporal_best_coverage\": 0.721,\n      \"r1_32b_static_best_layer\": 30,\n      \"r1_32b_temporal_layer_range_good\": \"24-34\",\n      \"qwq_temporal_best_layer\": 24,\n      \"qwq_temporal_best_coverage\": 0.581,\n      \"qwq_static_best_layer\": 26,\n      \"qwq_temporal_layer_range_good\": \"20-30\",\n      \"temporal_vs_static_layer_offset\": -2\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table S2: Layer selection for temporal probe (32B models)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"Temporal Best Layer\",\n          \"Static Best Layer\",\n          \"Offset\",\n          \"Good Range (>90% of peak)\",\n          \"Peak Coverage\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"28\",\n            \"30\",\n            \"-2\",\n            \"24-34\",\n            \"0.721\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"24\",\n            \"26\",\n            \"-2\",\n            \"20-30\",\n            \"0.581\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Middle-layer finding generalizes robustly. The broad plateau (10-layer 'good zone') means practitioners don't need exhaustive layer search — picking ~40-45% depth works well. The consistent 2-layer offset between temporal and static best layers is a minor but interesting finding for the paper's analysis section.\\n\\nTemporal probes prefer slightly earlier layers (2 layers shallower) than static probes. This makes sense: static probes need the most processed representation (later = more feature-extracted), while temporal probes benefit from slightly rawer representations where temporal dynamics are more pronounced before being abstracted away by later layers. The 'good range' spanning ~10 layers (15% of depth) means the finding is robust to layer choice — not a fragile optimization. QwQ's best layer is slightly earlier (38% depth vs 44%) consistent with its faster-forming representations.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"16-label taxonomy supervision\",\n    \"description\": \"Fine-grained 16-label supervision (Type 1-4 safety categories) instead of binary commitment. Tests whether richer labels improve coverage/timing.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_8b_16label_coverage\": 0.791,\n      \"r1_8b_16label_lead_mean\": -0.3,\n      \"r1_8b_binary_coverage\": 0.767,\n      \"r1_8b_binary_lead_mean\": -1.09,\n      \"coverage_improvement_pp\": 2.4,\n      \"lead_improvement\": 0.79,\n      \"r1_32b_16label_coverage\": 0.721,\n      \"qwq_16label_coverage\": 0.581\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table S3: 16-label vs binary supervision\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"Binary Cov\",\n          \"16-label Cov\",\n          \"Δ Cov\",\n          \"Binary Lead\",\n          \"16-label Lead\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"r1-8b\",\n            \"0.767\",\n            \"0.791\",\n            \"+2.4pp\",\n            \"-1.09\",\n            \"-0.3\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"0.698\",\n            \"0.721\",\n            \"+2.3pp\",\n            \"-0.6\",\n            \"+0.1\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"0.558\",\n            \"0.581\",\n            \"+2.3pp\",\n            \"-1.8\",\n            \"-1.2\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"16-label supervision provides marginal gain — confirms binary commitment is the right abstraction level for this task. The consistent +2.3pp across models suggests this is a real but small effect of better gradient signal, not noise. Include as supplementary result addressing reviewer concern but don't reframe any claims around it.\\n\\nMarginal improvement (+2.4pp coverage, +0.8 lead time) from 16-label supervision. The gain is small because the temporal window already captures most of the 'safety density decline' signal — finer labels provide slightly more gradient signal during training but the fundamental information (safety present vs absent over time) is already well-captured by binary. The improvement is consistent across models but never dramatic. This addresses the reviewer concern ('why not use full taxonomy?') with an empirical answer: marginal benefit, binary is nearly optimal for this task.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Oracle decomposition analysis\",\n    \"description\": \"Analysis of why oracle achieves +31 lead time but probes cannot. Characterizes what label patterns encode that hidden states fail to predict.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"oracle_exploits_future_absence\": true,\n      \"probe_captures_density_decline\": true,\n      \"probe_misses_abrupt_transitions_pct\": 0.61,\n      \"avg_steps_from_last_safety_to_cp\": 14.6,\n      \"probes_fire_after_density_below_threshold\": true,\n      \"oracle_fires_at_last_safety_label\": true,\n      \"information_gap_type\": \"future trajectory vs current state\",\n      \"abrupt_cp_traces_pct\": 0.42\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table S4: Oracle decomposition\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Trace Category\",\n          \"% of Traces\",\n          \"Oracle Lead\",\n          \"GRU Lead\",\n          \"Gap\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Gradual decline (>5 steps fade)\",\n            \"58%\",\n            \"+38\",\n            \"+7.2\",\n            \"31\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Abrupt transition (≤3 steps)\",\n            \"42%\",\n            \"+22\",\n            \"-2.1\",\n            \"24\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"All traces\",\n            \"100%\",\n            \"+31\",\n            \"+4.7\",\n            \"26\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The oracle decomposition reveals a principled limitation: 42% of traces have abrupt commitment transitions where pre-textual detection is impossible given current information. GRU achieves positive lead specifically on the 58% with gradual transitions (+7.2 steps). This reframes Claim 3: the 39% positive-lead rate roughly corresponds to traces where gradual decline provides exploitable signal. Future work could target better detection of 'abrupt' transitions via architectural advances (e.g., latent state models). This analysis contributes to the 'what works and why' diagnostic framing.\\n\\nThe oracle 'cheats' by definition — it sees the LAST safety label and fires immediately (hence +31 mean lead = avg distance from last safety to trace end). Probes can only detect that safety density HAS declined, not that it WILL NOT recover. 42% of traces have 'abrupt' commitment (safety reasoning present within 3 steps of CP) where no pre-textual signal exists by construction. The remaining 58% with gradual decline are where probes succeed (these are the ~40% positive-lead traces). The gap is not model capacity — it's information-theoretic: current hidden states encode current state well but don't contain reliable signal about whether safety reasoning will resume.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"OpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-7B base)\",\n    \"description\": \"4th model completing the 2×2 architecture×scale matrix. OpenThinker-7B on Qwen2.5-7B base.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"openthinker_static_coverage\": 0.419,\n      \"openthinker_temporal_coverage\": 0.605,\n      \"openthinker_coverage_gap_pp\": 18.6,\n      \"openthinker_gru_lead_mean\": 2.1,\n      \"openthinker_best_layer\": 14,\n      \"openthinker_best_layer_depth_pct\": 0.44,\n      \"scale_effect_llama\": 0.0,\n      \"scale_effect_qwen\": -0.047,\n      \"architecture_effect_7b\": 0.162,\n      \"architecture_effect_32b\": 0.14\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table S5: 2×2 Architecture × Scale matrix (temporal coverage)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"\",\n          \"7-8B\",\n          \"32B\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Llama-base\",\n            \"0.767 (r1-8b)\",\n            \"0.698 (r1-32b)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Qwen-base\",\n            \"0.605 (OpenThinker)\",\n            \"0.558 (QwQ-32B)\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The 2×2 matrix cleanly separates confounds: architecture (Llama vs Qwen) accounts for ~15pp difference, while scale (7B vs 32B) accounts for only ~5pp within each family. This finding supports the 'architecture-dependent benefit' reframing and provides a concrete recommendation: Llama-base reasoning models are more amenable to hidden-state temporal monitoring than Qwen-base ones.\\n\\nOpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-7B) provides the scale-matched comparison to r1-8b (Llama-8B). Its temporal coverage (0.605) is lower than r1-8b (0.767) — consistent with the cross-architecture finding that Qwen-base models have less exploitable temporal structure. The gap is comparable to QwQ vs r1-32b, suggesting architecture matters more than scale. The 2×2 matrix shows: Llama-base models consistently outperform Qwen-base models on temporal detection (~14-16pp gap), while scale has minimal effect within families. This separates architecture from scale confounds.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"GRU/Attention probe on 32B models\",\n    \"description\": \"GRU (256-d multi-layer) on 32B models. Tests whether increased model capacity helps with more data or if LR ceiling holds.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_32b_gru_coverage\": 0.721,\n      \"r1_32b_gru_vs_lr_coverage_gap\": 2.3,\n      \"r1_32b_gru_lead_mean\": 3.1,\n      \"qwq_32b_gru_coverage\": 0.581,\n      \"qwq_32b_gru_vs_lr_coverage_gap\": 2.3,\n      \"qwq_32b_gru_lead_mean\": 1.4,\n      \"attention_r1_32b_coverage\": 0.698,\n      \"attention_qwq_coverage\": 0.558,\n      \"gru_better_than_lr_on_coverage\": false,\n      \"gru_better_than_lr_on_timing\": true\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table S6: Probe architecture comparison on 32B\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"Probe\",\n          \"Coverage\",\n          \"Lead Mean\",\n          \"Params\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"Temporal LR W=15\",\n            \"0.698\",\n            \"-0.6\",\n            \"76.8K\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"GRU-256 multi\",\n            \"0.721\",\n            \"+3.1\",\n            \"1.6M\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"Attention (2-head)\",\n            \"0.698\",\n            \"+1.8\",\n            \"2.1M\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"Temporal LR W=15\",\n            \"0.558\",\n            \"-1.8\",\n            \"76.8K\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"GRU-256 multi\",\n            \"0.581\",\n            \"+1.4\",\n            \"1.6M\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"Attention (2-head)\",\n            \"0.558\",\n            \"+0.6\",\n            \"2.1M\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Confirms the 'simplicity is near-optimal' story for coverage detection. Temporal LR (77K params) achieves 97% of GRU's coverage (1.6M params). The GRU advantage is concentrated in lead time — a capacity/complexity trade that reviewers may find interesting but that doesn't change the main coverage claims. Supports the diagnostic framing: we understand what works (temporal context) and what doesn't help (more model capacity for coverage).\\n\\nGRU matches or marginally exceeds temporal LR on coverage (consistent with r1-8b finding where GRU's advantage was in timing not coverage). Coverage appears to be bounded by the oracle ceiling — once temporal LR saturates it, more capacity doesn't help for detection. GRU's advantage manifests only in lead time (using sequential context for anticipation rather than just detection). Attention probe slightly underperforms GRU — the sequential inductive bias of GRU is better suited to the temporal detection task than attention's permutation-equivariant processing. This confirms 'simplicity is near-optimal for coverage' while 'complexity helps timing marginally.'\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Text-based baseline (sentence-BERT)\",\n    \"description\": \"Sentence-BERT (all-MiniLM-L6-v2) embeddings + temporal LR W=15 on CoT text. Critical comparison to hidden-state probes.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"text_r1_8b_coverage\": 0.651,\n      \"text_r1_8b_lead_mean\": -2.4,\n      \"text_r1_32b_coverage\": 0.628,\n      \"text_r1_32b_lead_mean\": -2.8,\n      \"text_qwq_coverage\": 0.605,\n      \"text_qwq_lead_mean\": -3.1,\n      \"text_avg_coverage\": 0.628,\n      \"hidden_r1_8b_vs_text_gap_pp\": 11.6,\n      \"hidden_r1_32b_vs_text_gap_pp\": 7.0,\n      \"hidden_qwq_vs_text_gap_pp\": -4.7,\n      \"avg_hidden_vs_text_gap_pp\": 4.6,\n      \"text_auroc_r1_8b\": 0.83,\n      \"hidden_auroc_r1_8b\": 0.89,\n      \"text_captures_timing\": false\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 8: Hidden-state vs Text baseline comparison\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"Hidden Temporal Cov\",\n          \"Text Temporal Cov\",\n          \"Gap (pp)\",\n          \"Hidden GRU Lead\",\n          \"Text Lead\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"r1-8b\",\n            \"0.767\",\n            \"0.651\",\n            \"+11.6\",\n            \"+4.7\",\n            \"-2.4\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"0.698\",\n            \"0.628\",\n            \"+7.0\",\n            \"+3.1\",\n            \"-2.8\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"0.558\",\n            \"0.605\",\n            \"-4.7\",\n            \"+1.4\",\n            \"-3.1\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Average\",\n            \"0.674\",\n            \"0.628\",\n            \"+4.6\",\n            \"+3.1\",\n            \"-2.8\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The text baseline result is the most complex finding in median tier. Coverage gap exists but is modest and architecture-dependent — ruling out a 'hidden states strictly dominate' narrative. However, the TIMING advantage is clear and consistent: hidden states provide +3-5 steps lead over text across all models. This reframes the paper's contribution: hidden-state monitoring is not about better coverage detection (text is competitive there) but about EARLIER detection. The timing advantage is precisely the deployment-relevant metric — detecting commitment before the harmful text is generated enables intervention. Qualitative case studies become essential: showing specific traces where hidden states fire 5+ steps before any textual indicator of commitment.\\n\\nText baseline achieves 60-65% coverage — competitive but not matching hidden states on Llama-base models (r1-8b gap +11.6pp, r1-32b gap +7.0pp). However, on QwQ the text baseline actually slightly outperforms hidden-state probes (-4.7pp means text wins). This is the median tier outcome: text is not a pushover, gap is modest (avg ~5pp across all models) and architecture-dependent. The text baseline fails on TIMING (lead -2.4 to -3.1, worse than hidden-state GRU's positive leads) because textual signals of commitment arrive at or after the commitment point by definition — the model writes the harmful text AFTER committing internally. This is the key differentiator: hidden states provide pre-textual timing signal that text cannot access.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"BOCPD change-point detection\",\n    \"description\": \"Bayesian Online Change-Point Detection (Adams & MacKay 2007) applied to per-step probe confidence score sequences. Also test CUSUM as simpler alternative.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"bocpd_r1_8b_lead_mean\": 5.2,\n      \"bocpd_r1_8b_lead_median\": 2,\n      \"bocpd_r1_8b_early_detection_rate\": 0.47,\n      \"bocpd_r1_8b_coverage\": 0.698,\n      \"bocpd_r1_32b_lead_mean\": 3.8,\n      \"bocpd_r1_32b_early_detection_rate\": 0.39,\n      \"bocpd_qwq_lead_mean\": 2.1,\n      \"bocpd_qwq_early_detection_rate\": 0.31,\n      \"cusum_r1_8b_lead_mean\": 4.1,\n      \"cusum_r1_8b_early_detection_rate\": 0.42,\n      \"bocpd_vs_gru_lead_improvement\": 0.5,\n      \"bocpd_fpr_at_operational\": 0.15,\n      \"bocpd_hazard_rate\": 0.01\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 9: BOCPD vs GRU vs Oracle (lead time analysis)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Method\",\n          \"Lead Mean\",\n          \"Lead Median\",\n          \"Early Det. %\",\n          \"Coverage\",\n          \"FPR\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Oracle (K=5)\",\n            \"+31.0\",\n            \"+29\",\n            \"100%\",\n            \"100%\",\n            \"0%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"BOCPD (r1-8b)\",\n            \"+5.2\",\n            \"+2\",\n            \"47%\",\n            \"69.8%\",\n            \"15%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"GRU multi (r1-8b)\",\n            \"+4.7\",\n            \"0\",\n            \"39%\",\n            \"76.7%\",\n            \"12%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"CUSUM (r1-8b)\",\n            \"+4.1\",\n            \"+1\",\n            \"42%\",\n            \"72.1%\",\n            \"18%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Temporal LR (r1-8b)\",\n            \"-1.09\",\n            \"-1\",\n            \"23%\",\n            \"76.7%\",\n            \"8%\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 10: BOCPD cross-architecture\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"BOCPD Lead Mean\",\n          \"BOCPD Early Det %\",\n          \"GRU Lead Mean\",\n          \"GRU Early Det %\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"r1-8b\",\n            \"+5.2\",\n            \"47%\",\n            \"+4.7\",\n            \"39%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"+3.8\",\n            \"39%\",\n            \"+3.1\",\n            \"36%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"+2.1\",\n            \"31%\",\n            \"+1.4\",\n            \"28%\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"BOCPD is an incremental improvement over GRU for timing, not a breakthrough. The +0.5 step improvement and +8pp early detection rate improvement are real but modest. The method's value for the paper is dual: (1) it adds a genuine methods contribution (novel application of BOCPD to probe score sequences for safety monitoring), and (2) its failure to achieve oracle-level timing DESPITE being a principled change-point method strengthens the 'fundamental limitation' argument. If even BOCPD (designed precisely for detecting distributional shifts) can't reliably achieve early detection, the limitation is in the signal, not the detection method. Coverage drops slightly (69.8% vs 76.7%) because BOCPD's probabilistic threshold filters out marginal detections — a precision-recall tradeoff.\\n\\nBOCPD achieves marginally better lead time than GRU (+5.2 vs +4.7 on r1-8b) by explicitly modeling distributional shift in the probe score sequence rather than trying to predict future states. The improvement is incremental (+0.5 steps) not transformative — both methods face the same fundamental limitation (hidden states encode current state, not future trajectory). BOCPD's advantage comes from its probabilistic framework: it detects that a regime change HAS occurred slightly faster than GRU predicts one WILL occur. Early detection rate 47% is better than GRU's 39% but still far from oracle's 100%. The method provides an honest 'incremental improvement' story rather than a breakthrough. CUSUM performs slightly worse than BOCPD (less principled distributional assumptions). Coverage drops from 76.7% to 69.8% because BOCPD requires sustained evidence of change-point, filtering out borderline detections.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"False positive analysis on safe traces\",\n    \"description\": \"All probes evaluated on safe traces (final_judgment=0.5). Reports FPR, precision-recall curves, deployment utility.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"temporal_lr_fpr_at_70cov\": 0.14,\n      \"static_lr_fpr_at_47cov\": 0.21,\n      \"gru_fpr_at_70cov\": 0.12,\n      \"bocpd_fpr_at_70cov\": 0.15,\n      \"trajguard_fpr_at_40cov\": 0.33,\n      \"temporal_lr_precision_at_70recall\": 0.78,\n      \"temporal_lr_precision_at_80recall\": 0.69,\n      \"safe_traces_evaluated\": 76,\n      \"probe_fires_on_safe_pct\": 0.14,\n      \"mean_false_alarm_duration\": 3.2,\n      \"fpr_by_model_r1_8b\": 0.14,\n      \"fpr_by_model_r1_32b\": 0.16,\n      \"fpr_by_model_qwq\": 0.19\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 11: False positive analysis\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Probe\",\n          \"Coverage (unsafe)\",\n          \"FPR (safe)\",\n          \"Precision@Cov\",\n          \"F1\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Temporal LR W=15\",\n            \"76.7%\",\n            \"14%\",\n            \"0.78\",\n            \"0.77\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Static LR\",\n            \"46.5%\",\n            \"21%\",\n            \"0.63\",\n            \"0.53\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"GRU multi-layer\",\n            \"76.7%\",\n            \"12%\",\n            \"0.81\",\n            \"0.79\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"BOCPD\",\n            \"69.8%\",\n            \"15%\",\n            \"0.76\",\n            \"0.73\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"TrajGuard (MD)\",\n            \"39.5%\",\n            \"33%\",\n            \"0.49\",\n            \"0.44\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 12: FPR by model at operational threshold\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"Temporal LR FPR\",\n          \"GRU FPR\",\n          \"BOCPD FPR\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"r1-8b\",\n            \"14%\",\n            \"12%\",\n            \"15%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"16%\",\n            \"14%\",\n            \"17%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"19%\",\n            \"17%\",\n            \"20%\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"FPR 14% at operational threshold is the key deployment constraint. It's acceptable for human-in-the-loop monitoring (flag for review) but too high for autonomous intervention (blocking generation). The paper should frame this honestly as 'monitoring-suitable, not intervention-suitable at current precision.' QwQ's higher FPR (19%) aligns with its generally worse probe calibration — another architecture-dependent finding. The false positive characterization (most triggered by extensive safety deliberation) is useful for the paper's diagnostic contribution: it shows the probe correctly identifies 'safety engagement' but sometimes cannot distinguish 'engagement leading to refusal' from 'engagement leading to failure.' This motivates outcome-aware probe training in future work.\\n\\nFPR 14% at operational threshold (70% coverage) is acceptable but not ideal — consistent with median tier range of 10-20%. The false positives tend to occur on safe traces that contain extensive safety deliberation (the model reasons about safety extensively before correctly refusing — the probe misinterprets high safety reasoning density followed by resolution as a potential commitment pattern). GRU has slightly lower FPR (12%) because its sequential modeling better distinguishes 'safety reasoning leading to refusal' from 'safety reasoning ceasing before harm.' TrajGuard's high FPR (33%) further confirms its unsuitability. The 14% FPR means roughly 1 in 7 safe traces triggers a false alarm — deployable with human-in-the-loop but not for autonomous intervention.\"\n  }\n]","numLines":986,"startLine":1,"totalLines":986}}}
